      "text": "The user wants to find the pair of residues that have the most severe steric clash in the given protein structure.\nA steric clash usually means two non-bonded atoms are too close to each other. I need to calculate distances between all pairs of atoms from different residues, and find the minimum distance. If this distance is less than the sum of their van der Waals radii (or a standard threshold like 2.0 or 2.2 Å), it's a clash. The question asks for the \"single most severe steric clash\", which implies the pair of residues with the shortest interatomic distance between non-bonded atoms.\n\nWait, let's look at the PDB coordinates.\nI will scan the coordinates to find atoms that are very close to each other.\nSince it's a short sequence (residues 1 to 89), I can manually check distances or look for obvious overlaps.\nLet's list the coordinates of C-alpha atoms or sidechain atoms to see if any are suspiciously close.\nActually, I can just compute distances between all pairs of atoms, but that's too many. I'll look for residues that are adjacent in sequence or close in space.\nOften, severe clashes are between side chains of residues that are close in the sequence but folded back, or maybe between main chain atoms if there's a modeling error. But usually, it's a sidechain-sidechain or sidechain-mainchain clash.\nLet's check the sequence: MSE-HIS-LYS-ASP-GLU-LEU-LEU-GLU-LEU-HIS-GLU-GLN-MSE-VAL-ASN-ILE-LYS-ASP-GLN-PHE-LEU-GLY-PHE-ASP-HIS-VAL-ASP-GLU-THR-ALA-PHE-ALA-ALA-TYR-GLU-GLU-LEU-ASP-VAL-GLU-PRO-SER-HIS-VAL-HIS-LYS-SER-LYS-SER-GLU-HIS-LYS-HIS-ALA-VAL-PHE-LEU-LEU-GLY-ASN-ALA-LEU-ALA-ALA-ALA-MSE-SER-GLU-ASP-GLU-PHE-SER-SER-ALA-GLY-ARG-ILE-SER-LYS-ARG-MSE-GLU-GLU-LEU-ALA-ASP-ASP-ALA-SER\n\nWait, the sequence is just a list.\nLet's look at the coordinates for close atoms.\nI will write a quick mental script or just scan for coordinates that are very close.\nResidue 1: MSE A 1\nResidue 2: HIS A 2\n...\nLet's check atoms with similar coordinates.\nI'll pi